// ASR rule activity — audit vs block — Lab 06
//
// The state of an ASR rule is encoded in the ActionType SUFFIX, not a separate
// field: "...Audited" = the rule fired and ALLOWED the action; "...Blocked" = the
// rule fired and STOPPED it. A hunt that filters `ActionType startswith "Asr"`
// without splitting on the suffix cannot distinguish a blocked attack from a
// merely watched one. Always separate them.
//
// Also (Lab 06 §7): locally-set ASR rules (Add-MpPreference) do NOT appear in the
// Defender ASR report — that dashboard is scoped to policy-managed rules. This
// query is the reliable source of truth for whether ASR is firing.
//
// TIMEZONE: DeviceEvents Timestamp renders in the portal's configured timezone.
// Reconcile to one zone before comparing to a local trigger time (see traps in
// configuration-inventory.md).

// --- All ASR activity, split by state ---
DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName startswith "lab-win11"          // adjust to your device
| where ActionType startswith "Asr"
| where Timestamp > ago(24h)
| extend State = case(
    ActionType endswith "Blocked", "BLOCKED",
    ActionType endswith "Audited", "audited",
    "other")
| project Timestamp, State, ActionType, FolderPath, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc

// --- Count blocked vs audited over time (the report's blind spot, done right) ---
DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName startswith "lab-win11"
| where ActionType startswith "Asr"
| where Timestamp > ago(30d)
| extend State = iff(ActionType endswith "Blocked", "BLOCKED", "audited")
| summarize Count = count() by State, bin(Timestamp, 1d)
| order by Timestamp asc
